<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lion Hunt of Ashurbanipal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as lion hunt reliefs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lion Hunt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> is a series of carved stone reliefs from the palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, dating from the seventh century BCE. They show King Ashurbanipal hunting lions, a royal ritual demonstrating the king's role as protector of order against chaos. The reliefs are among the finest works of Assyrian art and are now held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lion-hunt-of-ashurbanipal.jpg" alt="Lion Hunt of Ashurbanipal" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exhibition_I_am_Ashurbanipal_king_of_the_world,_king_of_Assyria,_British_Museum_(45972455081).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The reliefs were carved for the North Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> in about 645-635 BCE, late in the roughly 250 years over which Assyrian palace reliefs were made. They show a formalised hunt in an arena, where captured Asian lions were released from cages for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> (reigned 669-631 BCE) to kill with arrows, spears or his sword. They were probably once painted as part of a brightly coloured decor. Within perhaps 25 years of their making the empire had broken apart, and in 612 BCE Nineveh was sacked and burnt.</p><p>For over a millennium before, the killing of lions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> seems to have been reserved for royalty. A clay-tablet letter records that a lion found in a house in the provinces had to be trapped and sent to the king by boat. Some 200 years earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a>, who had his own lion hunt reliefs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>, boasted of killing 370 great lions with hunting spears.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The slabs, mostly of gypsum alabaster, come from more than one space, chiefly narrow passageways off the larger rooms, and some fell from an upper floor. Unlike the palace's war scenes, many fill the whole height of the slab. On one side of a corridor, soldiers form an arena with a shield-wall while a crowd climbs a wooded hill; the king makes ready in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, huntsmen with mastiffs and spears wait, and one scene shows 18 lions, mostly dead or wounded.</p><p>A second group, in three registers with smaller figures, came from the upper floor and a small gate-chamber. Here lions charge from their cages at the king on foot, and the king pours a libation over the dead lions. In one scene the same lion appears three times, leaving its cage, charging and leaping at the king. Some of this group are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and others survive only in drawings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuzd-rassam">Hormuzd Rassam</a> excavated the North Palace slabs in 1852-1854 and William Loftus in 1854-1855, and most went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, where they are shown in room 10a. Critics have always praised the realism of the lions, shown alive, dying and dead in a wide variety of poses, while the human figures keep formal profile poses. Art historians note that the pathos modern viewers feel was perhaps not part of the Assyrian response.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lion Hunt of Ashurbanipal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Palace reliefs depicting the king in battle, the hunt and ritual, such as the celebrated <b>lion hunt reliefs</b> of Ashurbanipal now in the British Museum, proclaimed royal power as at once military, cosmic and divinely sanctioned, the king acting as the earthly agent of the god Ashur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
